{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 6, "model_name": "google/gemma-3n-e2b-it", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a powerful ruler who governed the County of Tripoli from 1146 to 1193. He was a significant figure in the Crusades and a key player in the political landscape of the Levant during the 12th and 13th centuries. His reign marked a period of prosperity and expansion for the county though also faced considerable challenges.\n\n*Early Life and Ascendancy* Raymond was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli. He inherited the county upon his father's death in 1146.  He quickly consolidated his power navigating the complex relationships between the various Crusader states and local Muslim powers. His early reign was characterized by military successes and diplomatic maneuvering. He strengthened Tripoli’s defenses expanded its territory and fostered trade relations. He skillfully balanced alliances with both Christian and Muslim factions.  Raymond III's political acumen and military leadership established Tripoli as a respected and influential entity within the Crusader world.\n\nRaymond III's reign witnessed significant military campaigns. He led expeditions against the Muslim rulers of Syria and Palestine.  He successfully defended Tripoli from various attacks and maintained a strong military presence in the region.  He also engaged in numerous conflicts with the Seljuk Turks and other Muslim dynasties. His military victories helped to secure Tripoli's borders and protect its interests. He was known for his strategic thinking and his ability to adapt to changing circumstances on the battlefield.\n\n*Diplomacy and Foreign Policy* Raymond III was a shrewd diplomat. He maintained a delicate balance of power among the various Crusader states and Muslim entities. He engaged in numerous diplomatic negotiations seeking to secure favorable treaties and alliances. He often used diplomacy to avoid direct conflict and to achieve his strategic 